Sum of Compatible Numbers in Range I
🔎 Problem Recap
Given n and k, consider numbers in [n-1, n+k].

A number x is compatible with n if:

abs(x - n) <= k

(x & n) == min(x, n)

Task: Sum all compatible numbers.

🧠 My Approach
Initially wrote:

cpp
if ((x & n) != 0) continue;
❌ Too restrictive — excluded valid numbers.

Correct condition is:

cpp
if ((x & n) == min(x, n))
✅ Ensures smaller number’s bits are contained in larger.

⚠️ Gap Analysis
Misinterpreted compatibility as “no overlapping bits.”

True definition: “smaller number fully contained in larger’s bits.”

Missed test case n=4, k=89 → got 2181 instead of 2184.

🟢 Brute Force Code
cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int sumOfGoodIntegers(int n, int k) {
        int ans = 0;
        for (int x = n-1; x <= n+k; x++) {
            if (abs(n - x) <= k && ((x & n) == min(x, n))) {
                ans += x;
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
🧠 Memory Hook
Compatibility = containment.

Don’t confuse with disjoint bits.

If you see (x & n) == min(x, n), recall: smaller fits inside bigger’s bits.

